id: pm-a5w7vv
title: "As an operator, I can ask pm a question in plain prose and receive the exact structured query plus its bounded answer, so insight never requires memorizing the flag grammar"
description: "Exploration story (spike + ADR only). Natural-language intent compilation: map a prose question ('what blocked the release last week', 'which items has this agent touched') onto the structured query surface (list/search/graph filters) with the compiled query always shown, deterministic, and inspectable. The compiled query is the product: NL is an input convenience, never a hidden second query engine. Acceptance of any implementation slice is measured by the retrieval quality contract (pm-p1g43q), not by prose plausibility. Horizon: far (4+), after the grammar freeze (pm-agou) so the compilation target is stable."
type: Story
status: open
priority: 4
tags[7]: adr-needed,agent-ux,exploration,"horizon:far",nl-intent,search,spike
created_at: "2026-07-26T22:34:25.668Z"
updated_at: "2026-07-27T12:58:21.716Z"
author: "harness:opencode"
estimated_minutes: 480
acceptance_criteria: "The spike produces an ADR with a recorded decision and rejected alternatives, not an implementation; The compiled structured query is always shown alongside the answer and is executable verbatim by hand, so natural language is an input convenience and never a hidden second query engine; Compilation is deterministic for a given workspace and question, and non-determinism is a defect rather than an accepted property; Quality is measured by the retrieval quality contract rather than by prose plausibility, and the ADR states the acceptance threshold before any implementation slice starts; The ADR states which grammar version the compiler targets and why the work is sequenced after the grammar freeze; A refusal path is specified for questions the compiler cannot map, so an unmappable question yields an explicit decline rather than a plausible wrong query"
parent: pm-ydp6
risk: high
confidence: low
